                "asynchronous_ai_overview": false,
                "markdown": "Dalet (ד) is the [fourth letter of the Hebrew alphabet](https://www.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/137076/jewish/Dalet.htm) , corresponding to the English letter \"D\" and carrying a numerical value (gematria) of 4.[[1]](https://www.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/137076/jewish/Dalet.htm)[[2]](https://www.7dorim.com/en/articles/articles-the-power-of-hebrew-letters/the-power-of-dalet-%D7%93/)\n\n### Key Characteristics\n\n- **Sound:** Pronounced like the \"d\" in \"door\".\n- **Meaning:** The name *dalet* comes from *delet* , which means \"door\".\n- **Symbolism:** It represents a doorway, humility (*dal* meaning a poor or low person), and the choice to step from one state to another.\n- **Evolution:** Its ancient pictograph shape resembled a tent flap or house door, which later influenced the Greek letter Delta (Δ) and the Latin letter D.[[1]](https://hebrew4christians.com/Grammar/Unit_One/Aleph-Bet/Dalet/dalet.html)[[2]](https://www.etz-hayim.com/hebrew/letters_and_vowels/dalet.php)[[3]](https://www.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/137076/jewish/Dalet.htm)[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dalet)[[5]](https://www.ancient-hebrew.org/ancient-alphabet/dalet.htm)\n\nWould you like to explore **gematria connections** for the number 4 or learn about **how Dalet is written in script and print**?",
